---
/**
 * For teachers, youth workers and librarians.
 *
 * Three reviews asked for a school route and they were right: /parents is written for a
 * parent, /schools/privacy is written for an IT reviewer, and a teacher opening this site
 * for the first time had to work out which of the two was nearer. Neither answers the
 * question a teacher actually has, which is "can I photocopy this on Monday and what do I
 * need to check first".
 *
 * What this page does NOT do: invent a poster format. A single Reality Check prints at 1.8
 * A4 pages, because the print rules keep the whole check and the non-affiliation line, and
 * squeezing it onto one sheet would mean cutting safety text to fit a layout. So the print
 * routes named here are the ones that exist and are measured, not aspirational ones.
 *
 * The licence split is the real story for this audience and it already exists in /terms:
 * everything is copyable, but the nineteen Reality Checks are the one thing that may not be
 * edited. A safety warning with a teacher's edits and Ryan's name on it is the only object
 * this project can produce that could genuinely hurt somebody.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Three things this page carries that nothing else on the site does.
 *
 * 1. THE RUBRIC. Schools do not buy motivation, they buy material they can assess, and the
 *    page had no assessment language at all. The six criteria below are the ordinary ones —
 *    problem clarity, skill practice, evidence, acting on feedback, safety decisions,
 *    reflection — and every one of them already has an artefact on this site, because a
 *    receipt IS the evidence a rubric asks for. Nothing new had to be built for it; the
 *    mapping was the work.
 *
 *    Its levels describe the ARTEFACT, never the student, and there are no rank names.
 *    "It exists" is a fact about a thing in a folder. A grade band would be non-negotiable 3
 *    wearing a mortarboard. The racing flags are deliberately NOT reused as a scale either:
 *    green/yellow/red means "who has to be in the room", and a scale that borrowed those
 *    three colours for quality would quietly break the one atom the whole site rests on.
 *
 * 2. THE CERTIFICATE. A site-issued certificate of completion was rejected — it is a badge
 *    with a longer name. What is here instead is a blank sheet a teacher fills in and signs,
 *    which is ordinary classroom practice and does not tie a reader's worth to a website's
 *    approval. It reads no storage, it is identical for every visitor, and it says so on its
 *    own foot line.
 *
 * 3. FINDABILITY. A teacher searching "entrepreneurship", "digital skills" or "money
 *    mindset" could not reach this page: the title said "For teachers" and the body was
 *    about photocopier logistics. The vocabulary added below is the book's own — printed
 *    page 63 names websites, future skills, entrepreneurship and "from consuming to
 *    creating"; printed page 3 names goals, skills, decisions, persistence, teamwork,
 *    attention and basic money habits. No curriculum standard is invented and no framework
 *    alignment is claimed, because neither would be true.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Print. This page deliberately prints as exactly two sheets — the rubric and the blank
 * certificate — and nothing else. The Q&A, the print index and the licence are screen
 * material for a teacher planning; five pages of them ahead of the sheet somebody actually
 * wanted to mark up is not a print stylesheet, it is a dump. Both sheets carry their own
 * source and licence line so a photocopy still says where it came from.
 */
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import ChapterFurniture from "../components/ChapterFurniture.astro";
import Box from "../components/Box.astro";
import Flag from "../components/Flag.astro";
import { totals } from "../lib/content";
import { FLAG_RULE_PAGE } from "../lib/build-meta";
import { SITE_HOST } from "../lib/site";

const t = await totals();

/**
 * What this material actually is, in the words a teacher would type into a search box.
 *
 * Every topic below is a description of content that exists, mapped to where it exists. It
 * is not a syllabus mapping and the page says so underneath: claiming alignment with a
 * named framework nobody has checked would be the same defect as an unsourced number.
 */
const COVERS = [
  {
    topic: "Entrepreneurship and enterprise",
    body: "Finding a problem worth solving, turning a skill into one clear offer, scope, deadlines and starter pricing set with an adult.",
    where: [
      { href: "/worksheet/first-offer-canvas", label: "Your First Offer Canvas" },
      { href: "/offers", label: "Seven Safe First Offers" },
      { href: "/path/ninety-day", label: "The 90-day path" },
    ],
  },
  {
    topic: "Digital and future skills",
    body: "Websites, AI tools, editing, design and research — used to make something, not to consume something. The book's own phrase for this is moving “from consuming to creating”.",
    where: [
      { href: "/c/04", label: "Chapter 4 — Level Up" },
      { href: "/c/14", label: "Chapter 14 — Mindful Tech" },
      { href: "/builds", label: `All ${t.builds} builds` },
    ],
  },
  {
    topic: "Money habits and money mindset",
    body: "Where money actually goes, the difference between revenue and profit, splitting what you earn, and why a young person's time is real.",
    where: [
      { href: "/c/11", label: "Chapter 11 — Money Mindset" },
      { href: "/c/15", label: "Chapter 15 — Real Rich" },
    ],
  },
  {
    topic: "Online safety, scams and pressure",
    body: `The ${t.realityChecks} Reality Checks are the safety layer: what a fake offer looks like, what secrecy means, and why an adult review is a test a real opportunity survives.`,
    where: [
      { href: "/reality-check", label: `All ${t.realityChecks} Reality Checks` },
      { href: "/race-control", label: "Green, yellow, red" },
      { href: "/tools/scam-check", label: "Scam Check" },
    ],
  },
  {
    topic: "Decision-making and risk",
    body: "One rule for anything hard to undo or landing on somebody else, three questions, and the habit of naming what you decided not to do.",
    where: [
      { href: "/c/07", label: "Chapter 7 — Boss Decisions" },
      { href: "/race-control", label: "The traffic-light rule" },
    ],
  },
  {
    topic: "Working with people, and sticking at it",
    body: "Asking a real person what they need, reading a room, recovering from a no, and keeping a promise for seven days without a streak that punishes you for missing one.",
    where: [
      { href: "/c/09", label: "Chapter 9 — Squad Goals" },
      { href: "/c/08", label: "Chapter 8 — Unstoppable" },
      { href: "/c/12", label: "Chapter 12 — Vibe Check" },
    ],
  },
];

/**
 * The rubric.
 *
 * Six criteria, each mapped to an artefact this site already produces. Two written levels
 * per criterion; the third level is "nothing to look at yet" and needs no words, because a
 * missing artefact describes itself.
 *
 * `changed` is always about what the work did, never about how the student seemed. That is
 * the only way this stays assessable from a folder rather than from a mood.
 */
const RUBRIC = [
  {
    name: "Problem clarity",
    question: "Can they say who it is for, and what was wrong before they started?",
    sources: [{ href: "/worksheet/first-offer-canvas", label: "Your First Offer Canvas" }],
    note: "The fields WHO I HELP, THE PROBLEM and THE RESULT. Book page 58.",
    exists: "All three fields are filled in, in their own words.",
    changed:
      "The person named is one specific group rather than “everyone”, and the result is something you could photograph.",
  },
  {
    name: "Skill practice",
    question: "Did they practise a skill, or read about one?",
    sources: [{ href: "/builds", label: `The ${t.builds} builds` }],
    note: "Each build is one short piece of work with something at the end of it — twenty minutes to an evening. Mark from the printed folder index, not from the ticks on a screen: ticks are kept in one browser and a school laptop clears them at sign-out.",
    exists: "At least one build is finished and the thing it asked for exists.",
    changed:
      "The same skill turns up in two builds, and the later one is visibly better than the earlier one.",
  },
  {
    name: "Evidence",
    question: "Is there proof, or a description of proof?",
    sources: [{ href: "/receipts", label: "The REAL RICH RECEIPTS folder index" }],
    note: "Build 01 is starting the physical folder. The printable index is the cover sheet that goes in the front of it.",
    exists: "The folder exists and has a line per build naming what is inside it.",
    changed:
      "The folder holds the thing itself — a file, a page, a printout, a message somebody sent back — not a plan to make it.",
  },
  {
    name: "Acting on feedback",
    question: "Did they ask a real person, and then change something because of the answer?",
    sources: [
      { href: "/path/tiny-launch", label: "The 30-Day Tiny Launch" },
      { href: "/path/ninety-day", label: "The 90-day path" },
    ],
    note: "Both plans build the asking in: “Ask ten people about the problem”, “Finish and ask for honest feedback”.",
    exists: "They asked somebody outside the room, and can say what that person actually said.",
    changed:
      "Two versions are in the folder, and they can point at the line that changed and name who caused it.",
  },
  {
    name: "Safety decisions",
    question: "Do they know which of their own decisions needed an adult — and did they get one?",
    sources: [
      { href: "/race-control", label: "The green, yellow, red rule" },
      { href: "/reality-check", label: `The ${t.realityChecks} Reality Checks` },
    ],
    note: "Plus the STOP RULES and SAFE CONTACT ROUTE fields on the First Offer Canvas. The rule is printed on book page 61; the flag on each build is this site applying it.",
    flags: true,
    exists: "They can say which colour their build was, and why it was that one.",
    changed:
      "A yellow or red decision genuinely waited for an adult, and they can name the adult and roughly when. Being able to name one thing they decided not to do counts double here.",
  },
  {
    name: "Reflection",
    question: "Can they say what did not work, without being asked to sound positive about it?",
    sources: [{ href: "/c/01", label: "The debrief questions" }],
    note: `Three at the end of every chapter, ${t.debriefQuestions} in all — on each chapter page, /c/01 to /c/${String(t.chapters).padStart(2, "0")}.`,
    exists: "The questions are answered in their own words rather than the book's.",
    changed:
      "One answer names something that failed, and a later build shows they did something about it.",
  },
];

/** Print routes that exist today. Each one was opened in print emulation and measured. */
const PRINTABLE = [
  {
    href: "/teen-and-grow-rich.pdf",
    label: "The whole book, as a PDF",
    /* This row used to say "put it on a shared drive, or send it to a class — the licence
       below allows all three". The licences below are the two Creative Commons grants and
       "the book itself is all rights reserved", so they allowed none of it. The book is
       free to read and free to download; it is not licensed to pass on as a file. Nothing
       is lost by sending the link instead — it reaches the same class and stays current. */
    note: "66 pages, 2.6 MB, the same file the print run was set from. Free, no email address, no sign-up. Read it, print a copy for yourself, set it as class reading. Send the class the link rather than the file: the book is free to read, but unlike everything else here it is not under a Creative Commons licence.",
  },
  {
    href: "#rubric",
    label: "The rubric, on one sheet",
    note: "On this page, below. Six criteria, each pointing at something the student already has to show you. Prints with tick boxes and a notes line.",
  },
  {
    href: "#certificate",
    label: "A blank certificate for you to sign",
    note: "On this page, below. Nothing on it is filled in by us — that is the whole point of it.",
  },
  {
    href: "/reality-check",
    label: "All 19 Reality Checks",
    note: "One page, every check in full, grouped by the risk it is about. The longest print here — check your page count before you send it to a shared printer.",
  },
  {
    href: "/race-control",
    label: "The traffic-light rule",
    note: "Green, yellow, red, with examples and the three questions. Three colours become three edge weights and three edge styles on paper, so it still works in black and white.",
  },
  {
    href: "/receipts",
    label: "The folder index",
    note: "A blank cover sheet for the REAL RICH RECEIPTS folder: 19 builds, 4 worksheets, 2 plans, a box and a line each. Prints the same whether or not the browser holds anything.",
  },
  {
    href: "/worksheet/starting-point",
    label: "Your Starting Point",
    note: "Seven fields. Prints blank and fills in by hand — no device needed at all.",
  },
  {
    href: "/worksheet/first-offer-canvas",
    label: "Your First Offer Canvas",
    note: "The one that turns a skill into something a real person could actually buy.",
  },
  {
    href: "/path/ninety-day",
    label: "The 90-day path",
    note: "Thirteen dated actions in three phases, three of them flagged red.",
  },
  {
    href: "/path/tiny-launch",
    label: "The 30-Day Tiny Launch",
    note: "Twenty-one day-tasks across four weeks.",
  },
];

/** What a teacher has to know before Monday, in the order it matters. */
const BEFORE = [
  {
    q: "Do students need an account?",
    a: "No. There is no account, no login, no email field and nowhere on this site to type a name. A class can use all of it without anyone signing up for anything.",
  },
  {
    q: "What does it collect?",
    a: "Nothing. No analytics, no cookies, no third-party requests, no server-side storage. Progress is kept in the browser on that device and never sent anywhere. Open developer tools, reload, and check the network tab — it takes about two minutes.",
  },
  {
    q: "What happens on a shared school laptop?",
    a: "Whatever a student ticks or writes is cleared when the device signs them out. That is why every worksheet prints and every page has a copy-as-text route: the paper is the record, and the site says so to students rather than pretending otherwise.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I photocopy it?",
    a: "Every worksheet, build, template and Reality Check, yes, and you do not need to ask. See the licences below — the only restriction is that the Reality Checks may be copied but not altered. The book's own text is the exception: free to read and free to download, but send the link rather than run off copies.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is anything blocked behind a purchase?",
    a: "No, and that now includes the book itself. The complete text — all fifteen chapters with their stories, Hill Remix essays and What It Means Now — is free to read on this site, and downloadable as a PDF with nothing to fill in. You can set it as class reading without anybody buying anything.",
  },
  {
    q: "So what is the printed copy for?",
    a: "The object. Something to hand to a student, leave on a shelf, or work through without a notification closing it. If you want a set for a classroom, ask — the mailbox is in the footer.",
  },
];
---

<Page
  title="For teachers: entrepreneurship and digital skills"
  description={`Free classroom material on entrepreneurship, digital skills and money habits for teens: ${t.builds} short builds, a printable rubric, a certificate you sign, no accounts.`}
>
  <ChapterFurniture
    eyebrow="For teachers, youth workers and librarians"
    title="Using this with a class"
    subtitle="Entrepreneurship, digital skills and money habits — as short builds a class can actually finish."
  />

  <div class="wrap" data-print="hide">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        <b>The complete book is free</b> — readable on this site and downloadable as a PDF,
        with nothing to sign up for. Everything else here is free too, nothing is locked,
        and you do not need to ask permission to print any of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Fifteen chapters, {t.builds} short builds, {t.realityChecks} Reality Checks and a rubric
        that marks the work a student already has to show you. Written for ages 13 and up, in
        English, by a thirteen-year-old.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="covers" data-print="hide">
    <h2 id="covers">What it covers</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Enterprise and entrepreneurship with a money-habits and digital-skills spine, shaped
        as practical tasks rather than lessons. The book's own words for its subject, on
        printed page 63, are websites, future skills, entrepreneurship, and helping young
        people move “from consuming to creating”.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="covers">
      {
        COVERS.map((c) => (
          <li class="covers__item">
            <p class="covers__topic">{c.topic}</p>
            <p class="covers__body">{c.body}</p>
            <p class="covers__where">
              {c.where.map((w, i) => (
                <>
                  {i > 0 && <span class="covers__sep" aria-hidden="true"> · </span>}
                  <a href={w.href}>{w.label}</a>
                </>
              ))}
            </p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p class="qa__more">
      That is a description of the content, not an accreditation. This material is not
      written to a national curriculum and does not claim to match one — if it fits a scheme
      of work you are running, that is your judgement to make, and the licence below lets you
      cut it up to fit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="before" data-print="hide">
    <h2 id="before">Before you use it</h2>
    <dl class="qa">
      {
        BEFORE.map((r) => (
          <div class="qa__row">
            <dt class="qa__q">{r.q}</dt>
            <dd class="qa__a">{r.a}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p class="qa__more">
      The full technical version, written for an IT reviewer rather than a teacher, is on{" "}
      <a href="/schools/privacy">the schools privacy page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="print" data-print="hide">
    <h2 id="print">What prints</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Print stylesheets here are written, not left to the browser — several of these exist
        specifically to be printed and worked on with a pen. Open the page and use your
        browser's print command.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="printable">
      {
        PRINTABLE.map((p) => (
          <li class="printable__item">
            <a class="printable__link" href={p.href}>{p.label}</a>
            <span class="printable__note">{p.note}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p class="qa__more">
      There is no single “print the whole site” button, and that is deliberate: it would be
      well over a hundred pages, and almost none of it is what any one lesson needs. Printing{" "}
      <em>this</em> page gives you two sheets and nothing else — the rubric and the blank
      certificate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="licence" data-print="hide">
    <h2 id="licence">What you may do with it</h2>

    <div class="lic">
      <Box kind="build">
        <p class="lic__h">Worksheets, builds, templates and tools</p>
        <p class="lic__l">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</p>
        <p>
          Copy them, hand them out, put them in a lesson, translate them, cut them up and
          rebuild them for your group. Credit the book, do not sell them, and keep whatever
          you make from them open under the same terms.
        </p>
      </Box>

      <Box kind="check">
        <p class="lic__h">The 19 Reality Checks and the safety text</p>
        <p class="lic__l">CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 — no derivatives</p>
        <p>
          Copy them and hand them out freely. <strong>Do not edit them.</strong> This is the
          one restriction on the whole site and it is not about credit: an altered safety
          warning circulating with a thirteen-year-old's name on it is the only thing this
          project could produce that would genuinely hurt somebody. If a check is wrong for
          your group, print it as it is and say so out loud in the room.
        </p>
      </Box>
    </div>

    <p class="qa__more">
      The book itself is all rights reserved. Full terms on <a href="/terms">the terms page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="shape" data-print="hide">
    <h2 id="shape">How the material is shaped</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every chapter is the same five parts: one principle, five steps, one build that fits
        in a lesson or an evening, a Reality Check, and three debrief questions. That
        regularity is the useful bit for planning — {t.chapters} chapters, {t.builds} builds,
        {" "}{t.realityChecks} Reality Checks, {t.playSteps} action steps and{" "}
        {t.debriefQuestions} debrief questions, all in the same shape.
      </p>
      <p>
        The green/yellow/red rule on printed page {FLAG_RULE_PAGE} runs through all of it,
        and it is the thing most worth putting on a wall: green is do it, yellow is tell an
        adult first, red is an adult does it with you.{" "}
        <a href="/race-control">The version written for students</a> is one page and prints
        cleanly.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        The flag on each build is this site applying that printed rule, not a mark printed
        beside the build in the book. Every page carrying one says so. If you disagree with
        one of the nineteen, the printed rule wins — please tell us, the mailbox is in the
        footer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* The rubric.

      Nothing here is new material — every criterion points at a sheet, a tick or a folder
      the reader already has to produce. That is the whole reason this is cheap to add and
      honest to publish: a receipt already is the evidence a rubric asks for.

      The levels are facts about the artefact. There is no grade band and no rank name,
      because a website inventing one is exactly what non-negotiable 3 rules out — and the
      racing flags are not borrowed as a scale either, since green/yellow/red already means
      "who has to be in the room". */}
  <section class="wrap section rubric-section print-sheet" aria-labelledby="rubric">
    <h2 id="rubric">A rubric, if you need to mark it</h2>

    <div class="prose" data-print="hide">
      <p>
        Six criteria, and every one of them is assessed from something the student already
        has: a canvas, a folder, a tick, a flag, an answer. Nothing extra has to be made for
        the marking.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two levels are written out. The third is “nothing to look at yet”, which needs no
        words. They describe the <em>work</em>, not the student, on purpose — the book's whole
        argument is that proof is a fact about what exists, not a rank handed to a person.
      </p>
      <p class="sheet-actions">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--primary print-action" data-print-page>
          Print both sheets
        </button>
        <span class="sheet-actions__nojs">
          Use your browser's print command — File, then Print. You get the rubric and the
          blank certificate, and nothing else from this page.
        </span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="sheet-head">
      Teen &amp; Grow Rich — rubric
      <span class="sheet-head__fill">
        Student <span class="sheet__rule"></span> Class <span class="sheet__rule sheet__rule--short"></span>
        Date <span class="sheet__rule sheet__rule--short"></span>
      </span>
    </p>

    <ol class="rubric">
      {
        RUBRIC.map((r) => (
          <li class="rubric__row">
            <div class="rubric__what">
              <h3 class="rubric__name">{r.name}</h3>
              <p class="rubric__q">{r.question}</p>
              <p class="rubric__look">
                <span class="rubric__k">Look at</span>
                {r.sources.map((s, i) => (
                  <>
                    {i > 0 && <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>}
                    <a href={s.href}>{s.label}</a>
                  </>
                ))}
              </p>
              <p class="rubric__note">{r.note}</p>
              {r.flags && (
                <p class="rubric__flags">
                  <Flag level="green" size="sm" />
                  <Flag level="yellow" size="sm" />
                  <Flag level="red" size="sm" />
                </p>
              )}
            </div>

            <div class="rubric__levels">
              <p class="rubric__level">
                <span class="rubric__box" aria-hidden="true" />
                <span class="rubric__k">It exists</span>
                {r.exists}
              </p>
              <p class="rubric__level rubric__level--more">
                <span class="rubric__box" aria-hidden="true" />
                <span class="rubric__k">And something changed because of it</span>
                {r.changed}
              </p>
              <p class="rubric__notes">Notes</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <p class="sheet__foot">
      Teen &amp; Grow Rich by Ryan Rijvers. The criteria are ordinary classroom ones; the
      sheets, folders and flags they point at are all free at {SITE_HOST}. Copy this rubric,
      change it, use it — CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. The {t.realityChecks} Reality Checks are the one
      thing on the site that may be copied but not altered.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* The certificate.

      A site-issued certificate of completion was proposed and rejected: it is a badge with a
      longer name, and a rank invented by a website is worth nothing. What survives the
      objection is the ordinary classroom object — a blank sheet an adult fills in and signs,
      because the signature is the part that carries the meaning.

      It reads no storage and takes no props. Every visitor prints the identical blank page,
      which is what makes it honest: the site cannot know whether anybody did the work, and
      it does not pretend to. */}
  <section class="wrap section certificate print-sheet" aria-labelledby="certificate">
    <h2 id="certificate">The certificate this site will not issue</h2>

    <div class="prose" data-print="hide">
      <p>
        There is no automatic certificate here and there will not be. A website telling a
        thirteen-year-old their work was good is a badge with a longer name, and this book
        argues against exactly that.
      </p>
      <p class="certificate__line">
        <b>The site confers nothing. A teacher in the room does.</b>
      </p>
      <p>
        So the sheet below prints blank, identically, for everybody. You fill it in, you sign
        it, and it means something because you saw the work — which is the only reason a
        certificate has ever meant anything.
      </p>
      <p class="sheet-actions">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--primary print-action" data-print-page>
          Print both sheets
        </button>
        <span class="sheet-actions__nojs">
          Use your browser's print command — File, then Print.
        </span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="sheet">
      <p class="sheet__title">Certificate</p>
